Z||000002||Phil||10-10-2000||08:19 PM||||I was in AZ in June-dry heat huh?  Horse poopies it was #@%^%$ HOT ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||10-11-2000||09:58 AM||||My inlaws live in Phoenix, so I've spent quite a bit of time out there.<P>Nine months out of the year (mid-September through mid-June), the weather is beautiful.  The other three months, Phil is right - it's like living in the fires of Hell.  (Of course, in Ohio, mid-April through mid-October are nice and the other six months are cold, gray, wet, and miserable.)<P>"Dry heat" means that in Arizona, sweating actually cools you off - unlike Ohio, where sweating just makes you wet and miserable because it's so humid.  I went hiking up Squaw Peak on a 95-degree day and never really felt hot, but I sure did drink a lot of water. I don't use auto A/C in Phoenix until it gets above 90 or so - the dry air blowing through the car will keep you cool.  It's pretty comfortable out there as long as the temps are around 100 or lower.<P>My father-in-law's 86 SE V6 does fine all year round in the Phoenix heat - even with the stock thermostat.<P>I think it's a tossup as far as paint is concerned - Phoenix has relentless sun, we have acid rain.  But the desert is really hard on interiors - fading upholstery and cracking dashes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||10-11-2000||02:17 PM||||My brother and his wife live in Tucson. He said that mostly it's just very dry and very hot, except for what the locals call monsoon season. Several weeks of storms and flash floods. <BR>They don't use an air conditioner. They have devices called "swamp coolers". Can cool an entire house through evaporative effects, due to the low humidity.<BR>He said that stuff out there generally doesn't rust, but the wind storms will sandblast your paint and windshield.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
